NOTES={
1:'Auxerre Deux docteurs de la loi is the 97×130 cm oil canvas, 1835.1.1 / 835.1.1. Annibale Carracci attribution remains explicitly qualified, with the source full 1576–1625 period. The Carrache/Carracci comparison scope contains no same-title or inventory object; no definitive artist link or precise year is inferred.',
2:'Falconnier Lincoln freeing the slaves is the 80×24×24 cm plaster group, inscribed Falconnier fecit–1865. The source mentions a bronze reproduction at the New York Union Club; that is a related physical version, not the held plaster. One integrated group counts once.',
3:'Anonymous View of Saint-Julien Abbey at Auxerre is an eighteenth-century oil canvas, 87×78×3.4 cm, 1872.12.12 / 872.12.12. The local institution and generic-title comparisons contain no matching object; anonymity and broad creation period remain.',
4:'Anonymous Noli me tangere is the 28.8×24×0.8 cm oil painting on slate, 2013.1.33 / D.873.1.3 / INV 2000 / B364. The 2013 municipal transfer and old imperial inventory labels are preserved. Former Correggio attribution and BLOMAERT reverse label are comparison evidence, not current named authorship. All 25 exact-title leads have other makers, incompatible supports or chronology; the inventory INV 2000 collision is a much larger nineteenth-century Ziem oil canvas titled Fleurs et fruits. The second numeric collision is a Potémont paper etching. No same-object holding is overwritten.',
5:'Anonymous Louis XIII enfant et son gouverneur is the large 244.5×162 cm oil canvas, 1924.1.3 / 24.1.3, with source 1676–1725 creation period. Former La Fosse attribution remains historical. The catalogue compares the child figure to Rubens’s Louvre INV 1777, while distinguishing the left arm; that narrative is neither a same-object claim nor a creation date of 1610. Inventory collisions resolve to Blanche’s much smaller 1914 Henley regatta painting.',
7:'Victor Adam CHAP. XI / Des mémoires ! fi donc is explicitly a separated plate from Un an de la vie d’un jeune homme on the native museum page. The 35.4×26.2 cm sheet, Langlumé printing credit, Sazerac et Duval dry stamp and 1824 production date identify one print; the chapter wording does not make this a whole book or eleven artworks.',
8:'Alophe Léon Gozlan is the signed lithograph on China paper applied to wove paper, 28.7×20.6 cm, BAL 517. Aubert is the printer, not painter Augustin Aubert. The existing Rajon portrait C 53.049/185 is a different maker’s smaller etching with separately documented plate size; same sitter is not same physical work.',
9:'Jean-Baptiste Arnout Jardin des Tuileries N°10 is a colored and gummed lithograph, sheet 21.5×26.4 cm, BAL 744. The native page labels Paris as the series title and transcribes the Lemercier/Bénard and Hauser print credits. The series label does not merge this individual view with unrelated Paris works; Eugène Lemercier’s oil painting Marais is another maker and medium.',
10:'Jean-Baptiste Arnout Vue du Pont Royal is the black-and-white lithograph, 24×31.7 cm sheet, BAL 1128. The native page distinguishes the Paris series title, image dimensions and separate mount, and transcribes its N°6 and Aubert/Bénard credits. It is one independently inventoried print, distinct from the Tuileries view and Jules Arnout panorama.',
11:'Jules Arnout Panorama de Paris is the watercolor-enhanced lithograph, 21.7×26.6 cm sheet, BAL 1129, with Formentin and Hauser credits. Native object/image/mount dimensions are separate, not three objects. Jules remains distinct from Jean-Baptiste; the quoted novel’s December 1815 scene does not establish the print’s creation date, which remains the source nineteenth century.',
12:'Louis Desnoyers BAL 615 is the 29×20.7 cm black-and-white lithograph on newspaper paper. The museum credits Aubert only as printer and describes one seated portrait with separate image/mount dimensions. No designer or Aubert painter identity is invented; original nineteenth-century creation period remains.',
13:'Baugé / Delaunois BAL 577 is one 18.2×27 cm lithographed sheet showing Desbordes-Valmore and Bouffé, signed Baugé 1833 under each figure. Source creation and inscriptions agree. Two portraits on the one sheet do not create two catalogue records.',
14:'Bertall BAL 2010.19 is explicitly a subscription poster for the Houssiaux edition on the native page, printed in black ink on wove paper, 44×30.4 cm. It reuses a frontispiece and is signed Bertall/Dumont. It is not a bound book or the related original frontispiece; broad source nineteenth-century date is preserved without converting the edition’s 1853–1855 history into an exact object date.',
15:'Carey / Mangeon Henri Heine BAL 688 is an individually inventoried print on laid paper. The source records 12.8×18.5 cm with further image/mount measurements; no unsupported printing process is added. No title/inventory comparison identifies the same object, and broad nineteenth-century creation remains.',
16:'Chamoüin Fontaine de la place Louvois BAL 745 is the wove-paper engraving, 27×37.3 cm with separately recorded image and mount measurements. It is a distinct fountain view, not another Paris series or an architectural structure, with broad nineteenth-century source creation.',
17:'Jules Champagne Mme Junot BAL 572 is the 29.8×20.2 cm wove-paper lithograph. Philippe de Champaigne’s male portraits and Gautier’s generic female portrait records have different named makers/subjects and are not this print. The nineteenth-century date and supplied maker spelling remain.'
}
