NOTES.update({
6:'Ch. Philipon BAL 436 is a 32.2×22 cm engraving on fine paper applied to wove paper, with separately labelled image and mount dimensions. The native page transcribes Ferdinand sculpt. / Moine imp.; these names require supplemental comparison searches but are not expanded to guessed personal identities. The source creator field stays unknown.',
19:'Daniel Stern BAL 443 is a 23.7×18.5 cm engraved portrait on fine buff paper mounted to wove. The native inscriptions distinguish Christine Claire as designer, Léopold Flameng as engraver and Alfred Salmon as printer. The nineteenth-century period remains broad; the sitter’s pseudonym is not a second creator.',
20:'Dantan Jeune Théophile Gautier BAL 2011.2 is the signed 18×12.4×10 cm plaster bust. The native object record explicitly gives production 1839 and the physical Dantan signature, with the 2011 gift separately labelled acquisition. No conflicting later-cast statement or matching existing object is present; source object date retained without substituting acquisition.',
21:'Daumier Bugeaud BAL 1139 is the 32.9×24 cm newspaper-paper lithograph, with HD signature, 47 notation, Aubert credits and Assemblée législative I inscription. The individual caricature is distinguished from its Les représentants représentés series. Generic portrait leads in the target scope are different makers and sitters.',
25:'David d’Angers Théophile Gautier BAL 726 is the 19 cm plaster medallion, explicitly signed and dated David d’Angers 1845. The native production field agrees; the 1845 sitting letter is contextual support, not a substituted casting or acquisition date. No separate edition objects are inferred.',
26:'Delpech Gall BAL 435 is the 27.4×17.8 cm black-and-white lithograph on wove paper, depicting Franz Josef Gall. The source nineteenth-century period remains broad. Image and mount dimensions describe components of the one print.',
27:'Vue de Wierzchownia BAL 417 is the 32×44.5 cm black-and-gray lithograph of a manor across a river. The native inscription credits Napoleon Orda as draughtsman and M. Fajansa in Warsaw as lithographer; these additional spellings enter identity comparison without replacing the literal exported maker label or inventing biographies.',
28:'Main de Balzac BAL 126 is the 22.8×11.5×4.5 cm bronze assigned explicitly to Fonderie Godard with production 1970 on both national and native object records. The physical foundry-made bronze qualifies at the cutoff; the narrative about Balzac’s death is not substituted for its production date.',
29:'Gavarni Honoré de Balzac BAL 271 is the 29×23.5 cm print dated 1856. The native page distinguishes this etching from the circa-1840 drawing on which it is based, and records its 1979 acquisition separately. Gigoux’s painting of Madame Hanska is a different sitter, maker and medium.',
31:'Gavarni Un déjeuner. Au petit jour BAL 1998.1029 is the 33.2×25.2 cm lithograph with Souvenirs de carnaval N°6 inscription and Bauger/Aubert credits, catalogue Armelhault–Bocher 2022. The five revellers at breakfast are one composition, distinct from the cancan in Un menuet; the broad source nineteenth-century date remains.',
32:'Gavarni Une fiole... BAL 1998.1001 is the 32.8×24.5 cm lithograph with 43-48 and Le Carnaval à Paris 40 inscriptions, catalogue Armelhault–Bocher 422. The native production year is 1843; the drunken man on the table and observing woman identify the individual plate within the series.',
33:'Gavarni / Budzilowich BAL 270 is a 27.8×19.6 cm individually inventoried and mounted print with separate image dimensions, depicting Balzac at Clichy. It appeared in the 1846 Oeuvres choisies; retain the museum’s before-1846 production wording and unknown lower bound, rather than using the publication date as an exact creation year.',
34:'Gavarni Jenny Colon as Sarah BAL 704 is the signed 24.1×14.5 cm lithograph with Caboche printing credit and Le Monde dramatique inscription. The native production date is 1835. It depicts one actress with a jug, distinct from generic portraits of other sitters.',
35:'Gavarni Une loge à l’opéra BAL 625 is the 24.6×15.8 cm lithograph with a cut laurel-wreath watermark, Frey inscription and plate 115 notation. Five people occupy one opera box within one inventoried sheet; the broad nineteenth-century date remains.',
37:'Gavarni Petits travestissements BAL 1998.294 is one 44.6×32 cm lithographed sheet containing fifteen costume vignettes. Native inscriptions identify Turgis, plate 124 and the bilingual title. The fifteen figures do not create fifteen records.',
38:'Les Anglais du voisinage BAL 1998.48 is the 27.6×35.6 cm colored print bearing Gihaut Frères and La vie de château 2ème partie N°4. The native author field credits the publisher only. Eugène Lami is mentioned in the narrative and is included in supplemental comparison, but is not promoted into an asserted creator.',
39:'Lami La noce de la fille du fermier BAL 1998.47 is the 27.6×35.5 cm colored lithograph with Gihaut credits and second-part N°3. The dancing wedding party is a different scene from the neighboring prints in the series; one independently inventoried sheet counts once.',
40:'Gorguet Monument à Honoré de Balzac BAL 336 is a 30.6×22.3 cm paper print signed Aug. Gorguet 94, with production 1894. The depicted sculpture and its inscribed 1799–1850 life dates do not turn the print into a sculpture or supply its creation date.',
41:'Gorvel Honoré de Balzac BAL 339 is the 24×17 cm print inscribed G. Gorvel sc. 1930. The related daguerreotype BAL 93 is explicitly its model, not this physical print. Production and signature agree at 1930; the 1979 acquisition stays separate.',
42:'Grandville Et vous Honoré... BAL 598 is the signed pencil, pen and ink-wash drawing on laid paper, 16.1×13.7 cm, bequeathed in 1963. The related BAL 280 wood engraving has different physical medium and larger sheet size. The drawing, print and cited 1845 magazine illustration are not interchangeable objects.',
43:'Grandville / Dubochet Thé artistique BAL 280 is a 24.9×17.8 cm wood engraving on wove paper, with separate image and mount dimensions. The source circa-1846 wording is preserved using only its independent nineteenth-century bounds. It illustrates Jérôme Paturot and is physically distinct from the related pencil/pen/wash drawing BAL 598; historical exhibition dates make no current-display claim.',
44:'Hopwood M. Thiers BAL 862 is the 26.9×19.4 cm wove-paper engraving with Hopwood sc. and Furne publishing inscriptions. Existing Carrier-Belleuse M. Thiers 895.5.1 is a porcelain sculpture by another maker, not the same physical object.',
45:'Ernest Hébert Estelle Gautier BAL 1997.16 is the 39.4×29.5 cm charcoal, sanguine and white-chalk drawing, dedicated to Théophile Gautier. The native 1997 notarized donation is acquisition; nineteenth-century source creation remains. Ernest stays distinct from sculptor Pierre Eugène Emile Hébert.',
46:'Pierre Eugène Emile Hébert Buste de Balzac BAL 822 is the monumental 100×77 cm plaster, dated 1877 by the museum and donated by Librairie Paul Ollendorff. The native page explicitly distinguishes it from the terracotta for the Odéon. The earlier 1850 memorial proposal is historical context, not the date substituted for this plaster.',
47:'Honoré de Balzac BAL 282 is the 43.5×30.5 cm print attributed to Edmond Hédouin on the native page. That explicit qualification must be restored in the object-level maker label with both source versions retained. The partly expanded signature Ed. Héd[ouin] [18]58 remains literal evidence; broad nineteenth-century date and missing exported medium are preserved.',
48:'La mort du père Goriot BAL 398 is the 23.1×29.7 cm wove-paper lithograph credited to Imprimerie Rigo. The native inscription additionally names J. Noguez de Paris, used for supplemental comparison. Rigo’s oil painting La Mort du général Négrier has another subject and medium; the depicted literary death is not a dated historic event for creation.',
50:'Bernard-Romain Julien Honoré de Balzac BAL 288 is the 30.5×22 cm lithograph with Galerie du Cabinet de lecture dry stamp and Aubert printing credit. Its native description identifies the hors-texte plate for the issue of 15 March 1839, supporting the source production year. It remains one mounted print.',
51:'Lacauchie Emile de Girardin BAL 1997.5 is the 30×21.7 cm signed lithograph with Galerie de la Presse and Aubert inscriptions. The single seated portrait and separate image/mount measurements identify one physical print; no exact year is inferred from its inventory.',
52:'Lafosse David d’Angers BAL 568 is the 34.8×26.3 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove, inscribed LAFOSSE 1866 and d’après un daguerréotype. The 1866 print is distinct from the photographic model and David’s own sculptures; the sitter’s reproduced signature is not authorship.',
53:'Lami La promenade après dîner BAL 1998.46 is the 27.5×35.8 cm colored lithograph with Gihaut credits and La vie de château second-part N°2. The group walking with dogs differs from the wedding and visiting-neighbors plates, with its own inventory and physical sheet.',
54:'Lami / Villain La traverse BAL 1998.34 is the 27.2×34.2 cm colored lithograph dated 1828, showing men recovering an overturned carriage. The native sheet/image/mount measurements are separate and the 1835 Balzac quotation describes a literary parallel, not the print date.',
55:'La vie de château BAL 1998.32 is an independently inventoried 27.5×35.7 cm lithographed title sheet, with a 17.2×19 cm image and separate mount. Its inscription names Villain and publisher Lami Denozan with 1828. Eugène Lami and Claude-Ernest Lami de Nozan remain separate maker/publisher roles, and one title sheet is not the whole series.'
})
