NOTES.update({
56:'Lassalle George Sand BAL 723 is the signed 43×34 cm lithograph on thick paper, with a 37×26 cm image. Existing same-sitter leads identify Vieusseux’s oil painting, Julien’s much smaller lithograph and Couture/Manceau’s different 48.5×40.3 cm print. The supplied lithographer and physical sizes are preserved; no match is made solely from the sitter.',
57:'Lassalle Charles Nodier BAL 586 is the 30.8×23.2 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove, signed Emile Lassalle 1839. Native source date and signature agree; Aubert is the printer and the physical portrait differs from the George Sand sheet.',
58:'Le Poittevin Les diables de lithographies BAL 1993.16.1 is the individually inventoried lithographed title sheet with musicians and dancing demons, separately measured image and mount. The source omits measurement units, which remain unfilled. Circa 1835 remains qualified within the independent nineteenth-century period; Aumont, Tilt and Frey inscriptions provide supplementary maker comparison terms.',
59:'Le Poittevin Diableries N°13 BAL 1993.16.14 is the 31.4×51.3 cm lithographed sheet, watercolor-enhanced according to the native description, signed ELP and bearing multiple publisher/printer credits. Multiple vignettes remain one print. It differs from the title sheet in design and image size; circa 1835 stays qualified.',
60:'Legenissel Homme luttant avec des fauves BAL 1109 is explicitly a cut-out lithograph fixed to Japanese paper, 15×26 cm, with Legenissel.J signature. The partly illegible certification concerning the Lion du Désert edition is preserved without inventing a signatory. It is one mounted print, not four animal pictures.',
61:'Mme Emile de Girardin BAL 527 is the 24.1×16 cm lithograph with Les belles femmes de Paris and Lemercier/Bénard printing inscriptions. The printer firm is the only supplied creator; Delphine Gay and Girardin are alternate sitter names, not multiple works or new artist identities.',
62:'Liphart Portrait d’Emile Zola BAL 718 is the 20×13.1 cm yellow-paper engraving inscribed E. de Liphart fec. The source nineteenth-century creation period remains. Gautier’s unrelated female portraits have a different maker and sitter.',
63:'Llanta J. de Pongerville BAL 608 is the signed 27.8×21.7 cm lithograph on wove and Japanese paper, with Aubert/Junca printing inscription. The full sitter identity in the narrative is not authorship. The broad nineteenth-century source period is retained.',
64:'Marquet de Vasselot Monument à Balzac BAL 880 is the 62.5×30×20 cm terracotta with native production 1888. The narrative only probably identifies it as one of two maquettes mentioned to the committee in 1888; that qualification remains. It is neither Rodin’s final monument nor two inferred model records.',
65:'Martial Potémont Rue de la Tonnellerie BAL 1006 is one 49.7×32.5 cm etched sheet containing two street views, signed APM with Beillet printing credit. The 1866 in one caption is preserved as source text, while the exported creation period remains nineteenth century. Two vignettes do not create two artworks.',
66:'L’intérieur d’une étude BAL 1198 is the 25.4×33.9 cm colored lithograph on laid paper, Musée grotesque N°37, printed/published by Martinet. The additional G de Cari inscription is retained and added to comparison scope without inventing a designer identity. The wedding-contract scene is one print.',
67:'Maurisset Le galop infernal BAL 2007.3.1.26B is a 25×35.6 cm independently catalogued lithograph with a separate mount, signed MAURISSET and bearing La caricature 14 / Aubert inscriptions. The title of the publication remains contextual; the dancing couples form one image, and no bound-volume statement is present on the captured object page.',
68:'Mirbel / Benard Duc de Fitzjames BAL 881 is the 24.8×17 cm lithograph whose inscription dates La Mode N°442 to 25 April 1835. Mirbel pinxit and Lith. de Benard preserve the painter/printmaker distinction; native production is 1835 and no model painting is substituted for this print.',
69:'Monnier Chef de bureau / Surnuméraire BAL 805 is explicitly a newspaper cutting, 15.3×21.6 cm, with two signed vignettes and a separate mount. It is one surviving print object, not two records. The exported medium remains unknown rather than guessed from its paper cutting description.',
70:'Monnier J’veux pleurer... BAL 1998.118 is the 27.6×19.5 cm colored lithograph from Les Grisettes, with Ardit and Gaugain publication credits. The repeated Ardit export name represents two roles for the same supplied maker, not two people. Broad nineteenth-century creation remains; source publication 1827 is separately retained.',
71:'Monnier Récréations plate 17 BAL 1991.54 is one 23×31.3 cm colored lithograph containing three captioned scenes. Native Monnier/Bernard inscriptions and uncertain AM/paired-R stamp are retained; three scenes and three title components do not create three artworks.',
72:'Monnier La persuasion / L’attente du soufflet BAL 1991.40 is one 23×31.3 cm colored sheet, Récréations N°9, with Bernard and Giraldon-Bovinet credits. The two scenes differ from plate 17 despite the same sheet size; no precise year is invented.',
73:'Monnier Tu es bête Fanny BAL 1998.73 is the 30.7×22 cm lithograph of an embracing couple, with Bernard/Giraldon-Bovinet credits and native production 1827. It and BAL 1998.118 both say Grisettes plate 1 but have different captions, postures, printing credits and image dimensions, so a shared plate number is not an object identity.',
74:'Récréations title sheet BAL 1991.39 is the 19.6×27.2 cm colored lithograph showing an assemblage of props, with Monnier, Bernard/Delarue and Giraldon-Bovinet inscriptions. It is an individually inventoried title print rather than a whole series; the source nineteenth-century period remains.',
75:'Monnier Un propriétaire BAL 2012.1.13 is the printed country gentleman scene, Souvenirs d’artistes N°362, with Bertauts and H. Monnier lith. inscriptions. The exported 29.6×43 measurements lack units, which remain unfilled. The 2012 inventory is not a creation date.',
76:'Monnier Le surnuméraire BAL 1989.101 is the 23.4×30.7 cm colored lithograph of a grisette with a hairdresser, printed by Delpech. It is a different composition from the office scenes BAL 805 despite sharing a title word. Its separate sheet/image/mount dimensions and broad date remain.',
77:'Monnier Les paniers à deux anses BAL 1991.126 is the 26×34 cm colored lithograph, N°3, with Feillet printing credit and a verso G stamp. The man with two women forms a single composition on one sheet; the unknown stamp identity is not expanded.',
78:'Monnier Mamans de comédie BAL 1989.98 is the 25.7×33.7 cm colored lithograph, Galerie Théâtrale N°12, with Gaugain/Ardit addresses. Its group of older women conversing is distinct from the other selected Monnier scenes. The tentative identification of the room remains tentative.',
79:'Monnier Pauvre cousin regarde ton habit BAL 1998.59 is the 35.7×26 cm colored lithograph with Senefelder/Gihaut credits, showing an impoverished visitor before a seated fashionable man. The source nineteenth-century period is retained without deriving a date from Balzac’s quoted novel.',
80:'Monnier Un bel homme / La recherche de maris BAL 1991.41 is one 23.1×31.5 cm colored sheet with two scenes, Bernard/Giraldon-Bovinet credits and uncertain collector stamp. Distinct captions and figures separate it from the similarly sized Récréations plates; it counts once.',
81:'Monnier C’est encore moi... BAL 1991.75 is the 23×31.4 cm colored Récréations print of a visitor interrupting a seated couple, with an umbrella in the room. Native maker and printer inscriptions distinguish the individual plate; the uncertain collector monogram remains unresolved.',
82:'Monnier Les cochers BAL 2012.1.15 is the 33×25.2 cm print contrasting a hearse driver and cabman, with Monnier and Motte inscriptions. The related-work link and source nineteenth-century period are retained; the two figures are one artwork.',
83:'Monnier Récréations BAL 1991.44 is one 19×27.3 cm colored lithograph with four captioned vignettes, Monnier/Motte/Giraldon-Bovinet credits. The marriage, education, drinking and hairdresser scenes share one physical sheet, so it counts once.',
84:'Monnier Un pauvre diable paye ses bottes BAL 1998.69 is the 24.2×32 cm colored lithograph from Les Marionnettes, with Francisque Noël and Giraldon-Bovinet inscriptions. The misspelled Monner inscription stays literal; the boot-purchase scene is distinct from the other selected prints.',
85:'Monnier L’économie du fiacre BAL 2012.1.19 is a gouache-enhanced print bearing Senefelder et Cie and bilingual title inscriptions. Native dimensions anomalously give an image taller than the work; both measurements remain literal without repair. Reverse pencil numbers and the two pedestrians identify this one print, not a precise date or an expanded edition.',
89:'Léon Noël Lafont BAL 702 is the 28.3×21.5 cm lithograph signed Léon Noël 1837 with Bernard/Frey printing credit. Existing Bernard-name painter portraits are unrelated makers or sitters; the theater subtitle identifies Lafont’s role, not a second artwork.',
90:'O’Connell / Wolff Emile de Girardin BAL 887 is the 30×20.6 cm laid-paper engraving, inscribed Gravé par L. Wolff d’après Frédérique O’Connell and printed by Chardon. It differs from Lacauchie’s larger-image wove-paper lithograph BAL 1997.5 in maker roles, technique, support and portrait orientation. The O’Connell/Miethe identity spellings require the supplemental comparison scope.',
91:'Perrichon Barbey d’Aurevilly BAL 888 is the signed 28×21.2 cm wove-paper etching of the sitter in profile. Native nineteenth-century creation remains; no exact year is inferred from the artist’s lifetime.',
92:'Ratier Grandville BAL 441 is the 32.3×25.2 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove, published by Aubert with a Brussels Dewasme-Pletinkx credit. Grandville is the portrait subject, not its author; Ratier’s source label and broad date remain.',
93:'Roze Contrastes littéraires BAL 521 is a 24.4×15.1 cm cut print with truncated lower inscription, signed Roze and printed by Aubert. The native page identifies its publication in Le Monde dramatique in 1840; the broad exported nineteenth-century date remains. Balzac and Lamartine are depicted authors, not makers.',
94:'Régnier / Champin Balzac’s house BAL 313 is the 34×24.5 cm wove-paper lithograph with Thierry printing credits and BR/BN exchange stamps. The 1976 exchange stamp is provenance, while Balzac’s 1829–1835 residence describes the subject. Neither replaces the source nineteenth-century creation period.',
96:'Alphonse Louis Pierre Trimolet La rue Basse des Ursins BAL 1005 is the 47.3×31 cm etching, signed P. Trimolet and printed by Vve A. Cadart. The widow’s literal printing inscription is preserved alongside the exported Cadart label; this is distinct from Louis-Joseph Trimolet’s smaller Passy etching.',
97:'Louis-Joseph Trimolet Barrière de Passy BAL 560 is the 23.2×16.2 cm laid-paper etching with Trimolet sc. inscription. The maker’s full supplied name, different Paris view and physical support distinguish it from Alphonse Louis Pierre Trimolet’s Rue Basse des Ursins.',
98:'Jules Van Marcke Place du Châtelet BAL 761 is the 26.5×35.6 cm wove-paper lithograph, N°2, with Van Marcke, F. Noël and Giraldon-Bovinet inscriptions. It depicts the Châtelet and Conciergerie; separate creator/printmaker/publisher roles and the literal nineteenth-century date remain.',
99:'Vernier A la chaumière BAL 624 is the signed 36.8×24.1 cm lithograph with Aubert credits and Physionomies des bals publics title. The depicted dance hall’s operating dates are contextual, not a narrowed creation interval; it is one independently inventoried print.',
100:'Anonymous Ernesta Grisi BAL 1997.15 is the small 16×14 cm oil painting on wood, donated in 1997. Its nineteenth-century creation period and unknown maker remain; the sitter’s relationship with Gautier does not establish an exact painting year.'
})
