NOTES.update({
101:'Le château de la Chevrière BAL 446 is an anonymous 22.8×28.8 cm graphite drawing of a château among fir trees. Repeated work/image measurements describe one sheet with a separate mount. Source circa 1880 remains qualified within its independently given nineteenth-century bounds.',
102:'Alophe Méry BAL 580 is the signed 30.7×22.8 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove, printed by Aubert for Galerie de la Presse. The named sitter and different physical sheet distinguish it from Alophe’s other selected portraits; unrelated Gautier female portraits do not match.',
103:'Arnout Notre Dame N°9 BAL 1130 is the 21.7×26.7 cm colored and gummed lithograph of the cathedral façade from the square. Its Paris series title is not an individual-work match; the supplied maker and printing credits distinguish it from other Paris paintings and Lami’s interior reading scene titled Notre-Dame de Paris.',
104:'Frédéric Soulié BAL 616 is the 30.8×22.1 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove with Aubert printing credits. The personal designer is not supplied and is not inferred from the similar Galerie de la Presse portraits. Source nineteenth-century date remains broad.',
108:'David d’Angers Portrait de Balzac de profil BAL 98 is the 21×16 cm graphite drawing dated and dedicated to Madame de Surville in 1843. The native page explicitly distinguishes this preparatory drawing from the profile medallion, frontal medallion and marble bust; 1930 is its acquisition, not creation.',
110:'Gavarni Insulaire de n’importe où BAL 1998.1056 is the watercolor/gouache-enhanced lithograph, 32.8×25 cm, explicitly Souvenir du bal Chicard N°8. Its grotesque costume subject and plate number differ from the existing Pistolet and Mignon-Minard plates and selected Troubadour N°9. The historical title remains literal without endorsing its caricature.',
113:'Honoré de Balzac BAL 283 is explicitly attributed to Hédouin on the native page; restore that missing qualification with original export retained. This 43.5×30.8 cm etching has a 24.5×19.5 cm image of Balzac in an artist’s shirt and an incomplete mount dedication. It differs from BAL 282’s 12×10 cm profile image and partly legible 1858 signature.',
114:'Julien Balzac BAL 289 is the 26.4×19.6 cm lithograph for Galerie de la Presse, inscribed 1840. It differs from BAL 288’s Cabinet de lecture 1839 portrait in gaze direction, image size, lettering and publication. Philipon’s earlier request for a sitting is contextual rather than a replacement date.',
115:'Lami Notre-Dame de Paris BAL 1998.49 is an interior reading scene, not an architectural view. The 27.7×35.6 cm colored lithograph is inscribed Eug. Lami 1832 and second-part N°5. It is distinct from Arnout’s cathedral view and other same-title landscapes.',
117:'Le Poittevin Diableries N°9 BAL 1993.16.10 is one watercolor-enhanced lithographed sheet with six demonic scenes, ELP initials and Aumont/Formentin/Tilt credits. It differs from plates 10 and 13 in composition and plate number. Unspecified measurement units and source circa 1835 are not silently filled or made exact.',
118:'Liphart Théophile Gautier BAL 1997.14 is the signed 47.8×37.8 cm pen drawing dated 1879, after a Barenne photograph. The drawing remains distinct from that photographic model, and the 1997 donation is separately preserved. Supplemental Barenne comparison is required without changing the supplied maker label.',
119:'Monnier Henry Monnier BAL 1998.57 is the 35.7×26 cm brown-toned lithograph with seven vignettes around a self-portrait. Gavarni’s same-sitter portrait is a different maker’s isolated full-length figure; the seven-image composition counts once and remains distinct from BAL 438.',
120:'Monnier Récréations plate 25 BAL 1991.69 is one 23×31.4 cm colored sheet with two conversational scenes and Bernard printing credit. Its specific captions and 13×20 cm image differ from the other selected Récréations plates despite shared sheet sizes. Unknown stamp reading remains qualified.',
121:'Monnier Le petit bonhomme / Les amateurs BAL 1991.42 is Récréations N°6, a 23×31.4 cm colored sheet with two scenes and Bernard/Giraldon-Bovinet inscriptions. It represents one physical object; the literal nineteenth-century date remains.',
122:'Monnier Vous êtes tous des monstres BAL 1998.82 is the 30.8×22 cm lithograph, Les Grisettes plate 10, with native production 1827 and Bernard/Giraldon-Bovinet credits. The two women walking away from a man distinguish this from the other selected Grisettes plates.',
123:'Monnier Une méprise BAL 1989.114 is the 32.6×47.7 cm gouache-enhanced lithograph, Esquisses Parisiennes N°7, with Delpech printing inscription. It is one independently donated and mounted print; the quoted novel’s events are not object dating evidence.',
124:'Monnier Les paniers à deux anses BAL 1991.127 is plate 4, showing one woman taking the arms of two men. BAL 1991.126 is plate 3 with one man between two women. Different compositions and numbered inscriptions establish distinct works despite similar titles, sheet sizes and Feillet printing credits.',
125:'Monnier Les antipodes BAL 1998.61 is the 35.7×26 cm colored lithograph of two men back to back, one shabby and seated, the other standing in a fur-lined coat. Senefelder/Gihaut inscriptions and the individual title distinguish it from other identically sized series sheets.',
126:'Monnier Les extravagances BAL 1998.60 is the 35.7×26 cm colored lithograph of two men in exaggerated high-shouldered, tightly waisted fashion. Its distinct subject and 23.2×18.2 cm image identify the sheet alongside Senefelder/Gihaut credits; no exact year is invented.',
128:'Le vieil antiquaire BAL 2014.11 is explicitly a reversed brown-ink drawing after an anonymous engraving from the 1838 Delloye/Lecou Peau de chagrin edition. Preserve copy qualification and unknown personal maker. The 6.4×5.9 cm measurements refer to the image, not an invented sheet size; the exported nineteenth-century date stays broad.',
129:'Le Luxembourg BAL 746 is the anonymous 23.1×31.8 cm landscape-format engraving on China paper applied to wove, showing the palace and Saint-Sulpice towers. Existing Béjot Le Luxembourg is a 1903 portrait-format laid-paper etching with a much taller plate and black-paper mount, so it is a distinct physical work.',
130:'Alophe Emile Marco de Saint-Hilaire BAL 690 is the signed 31×22.8 cm wove-paper lithograph with Aubert/Galerie de la Presse inscriptions. The sitter, 23×15.6 cm image and own inventory distinguish this from Alophe’s other portraits.',
131:'Mme Emile de Girardin BAL 883 is the 31.1×22.8 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove, with a Julien signature omitted from the exported author field. Add Julien to supplemental comparison and retain the inscription as evidence. The three-quarter face, larger image and different print credits distinguish it from the frontal BAL 527 portrait.',
134:'David d’Angers Buste de Balzac BAL 188 is the 77.5×36×36.7 cm marble, 150 kg, dedicated and dated 1844 on its base. The January 1845 letter announces its completion/gift; both dates remain evidence while the museum’s 1844 production is retained. The tomb bronze and Saumur plaster are explicitly other physical versions, and Hébert’s monumental plaster is another maker’s work.',
135:'Gavarni Un bal à la chaussée d’Antin BAL 479 is the signed 19.9×26.2 cm lithograph with Frey printing inscription. Its crowded indoor ball composition and landscape image distinguish it from the individual costume plates of Bal Chicard.',
136:'Gavarni Troubadour BAL 1998.1057 is the 32.8×24.7 cm lithograph enhanced with watercolor and gouache, Bal Chicard N°9, with Aubert/Bauger credits. The dancing pipe-smoking troubadour differs from N°8 Insulaire and the named existing Pistolet/Mignon-Minard plates.',
139:'Lami La course en sao BAL 1998.50 is the 27.6×35.5 cm colored lithograph of a sack race, signed Eug. Lami 1832, second-part N°6. The literal sao title typo remains; seven racers on one sheet are not seven works. It differs from Lami’s existing large oil paintings of royal receptions.',
140:'Lami L’arrivée des lettres et des journaux BAL 1998.35 is the 27.4×34.9 cm colored lithograph, first-part N°2, with Villain/Lami-Denozan credits and 1828 production. The interior newspaper-and-letter reading scene is physically distinct from the existing small oil-on-wood L’Arrivée and royal-arrival paintings.',
141:'Le Poittevin Diableries N°10 BAL 1993.16.11 is one 34.4×51.2 cm watercolor-enhanced lithograph with six scenes, ELP initials and Aumont/Formentin/Tilt credits. Its plate number and specific compositions differ from N°9, N°13 and the title sheet. Circa 1835 remains qualified.',
142:'Monnier Les Grisettes BAL 1998.72 is the individually inventoried 29.9×22 cm lithographed title sheet, decorated with typographic lettering and scrolls, with a separate mount. The inscribed and native production date is 1827; it counts as one print, not as the entire series.',
143:'Monnier M. Emile BAL 1997.22 is the 40×31 cm bon-à-tirer proof with CHM oval stamp and handwritten instruction concerning 550 copies. The literal title Empire and inscription Empesé remain distinct source wordings. Only this proof is counted, without inventing 550 held impressions.',
144:'Monnier Le jour de l’an BAL 1991.51 is the 23×31.4 cm colored Récréations print of an employee offering a sugar loaf to his superior. Bernard/Giraldon-Bovinet credits and uncertain monogram/illegible plate number stay literal. It differs from the other workplace and conversation prints.',
145:'Monnier C’est pas l’amitié... BAL 1998.83 is Les Grisettes plate 11, 31.1×21.9 cm, with native production 1827 and Bernard/Giraldon-Bovinet credits. The woman with a hatbox passing a seated man is a distinct composition from plates 1 and 10.',
146:'Monnier Huit heures BAL 1991.25 is the 26×35 cm colored lithograph from Mœurs administratives, with Delpech printing credit and a 4 watermark notation. Its office-cleaning scene differs from BAL 805’s two contrasted office vignettes; the time of day is not a creation date.',
147:'Monnier L’économie du fiacre BAL 1991.128 is the 26×34.2 cm gouache-enhanced lithograph printed by Feillet, numbered 5, with verso G stamp. The related BAL 2012.1.19 has an explicit Senefelder et Cie printing inscription and substantially different image dimensions. Preserve both stated printing versions and the latter’s measurement anomaly without merging their physical records.',
148:'Monnier Une grande dame BAL 1998.62 is the 35.7×26 cm colored lithograph with Senefelder/Gihaut credits, showing a large fashionably dressed woman followed by her servant from behind. It differs from the other similarly sized fashion plates in composition and image measurements.',
150:'Vue des Champs-Elysées en 1789 BAL 1122 is the anonymous 21×29.5 cm laid-paper engraving, plate 61. The title’s 1789 dates the represented view; creation remains the museum’s nineteenth-century period. No maker is guessed.',
151:'Alophe Jules Janin BAL 709 is the signed 30.9×23.2 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove, printed by Aubert. Existing Schopin Portrait de Jules Janin is a much larger 1835 oil canvas by another maker; the shared sitter does not identify one object.',
152:'Eugène Sue BAL 439 is the 28.5×21.5 cm lithograph on China paper applied to wove, with Aubert printing and Galerie de la Presse inscriptions. No personal designer is supplied; the depicted novelist is not promoted to creator. Source nineteenth-century production remains broad.',
155:'David d’Angers Auguste-Marseille Barthélémy BAL 2001.13 is the 15.5 cm bronze medallion signed DAVID 1832 and inscribed with the sitter’s name. The native production field agrees; the 2001 purchase and Balzac’s 1831 letter are separately retained. No additional cast or edition size is inferred.'
})
