NOTES.update({
156:'Albignac Femme vue de dos K3935 is the 27×21 cm colored-pencil/graphite drawing, signed and dated 24 May 1927 on the native page. Renoir’s similarly sized oil canvas and Torrents/Gombert same-title works have different makers and media. The 1987 gift is separate from creation.',
157:'Journal des Demoiselles plate 5001, inventory 1981.138.46.8, is one 32.7×24.8 cm color intaglio print with three fashion figures. B.C remains initials, Deferneville is designer and the journal publisher; the native page records 1 August 1894 and a verso Don Vallier 1981 inscription. The figures are not three artworks.',
158:'Journal des Demoiselles plate 5266, 2004.666.4, is the 32.5×25 cm color lithograph natively dated 1 January 1902. B.C is not expanded to an invented personal identity. It is one print showing three dresses, not three garments or an entire journal volume.',
159:'Journal des Demoiselles / Petit Courrier plate 5259, 1962.78.1, is the 29×40 cm intaglio print natively dated 1 October 1901. Its Carnavalet stamp and Don Passerat 62-78-1 inscription are historical source facts; the current native collection explicitly names Galliera. Seven fashion figures share one sheet.',
160:'Baquoy / Le Clerc Galerie des modes plate 266, 2025.21.79, is explicitly a watercolor-enhanced print fixed to a separate mount, 30×19.9 cm. The native page documents its June 1983 movement from Carnavalet and March 2025 formal reassignment to Galliera, resolving the old Carnavalet stamp without claiming current display. Circa 1780 stays qualified; Leclerc comparison spelling must be included.',
161:'Berlier / Héloïse Colin-Leloir La Corbeille plate 818, 1982.152.20.49, is one 24.5×19.5 cm watercolor-enhanced intaglio print dated 1 August 1867. The two seaside fashion figures form one print, and Edith Brayer’s 1982 gift is acquisition rather than creation. Leloir comparison spelling is required.',
164:'Bouët-Willaumez 1986.93.13 is the 37×28.1 cm pen-and-black-ink drawing dated 1 May 1939, with native author notation and separate retailer/fashion-house references. Hattie Carnegie is a department store in the source role, not a second draughtsman. The four figures share one original sheet.',
165:'Bouët-Willaumez The Colony 1986.93.8 is the 41×32.5 cm watercolor/black-ink drawing signed RBW, with Vogue number and 2/15/39 verso date. Henri Bendel is the department-store clothing credit. The held original drawing stays distinct from its magazine publication and the represented garment.',
166:'Bouët-Willaumez Ensemble du soir 1986.93.10 is the signed 42.3×32.5 cm watercolor/ink drawing marked Vogue 15 September 1938 and Molyneux 1938. The native source separately cites Horst’s 1939 photograph of the same outfit; that photograph is another work and does not change this drawing’s date.',
168:'Héloïse Colin-Leloir K1567 is the 22.6×30.5 cm graphite, watercolor, white-highlight and ink drawing on card, signed Héloïse Leloir. Circa 1869 remains qualified using the source nineteenth-century bounds; the related preparatory drawing is not assumed identical from the relationship link.',
169:'Crozet Paul Poiret 2024.0.6 is the 24.5×16.2 cm graphite drawing on China paper mounted to a separate 35×27 cm card, signed and dated 27 June 1922. The 2024 retrospective inventory and Don K Lagerfeld mount notation remain provenance. The listed 2025–2026 exhibition is past display evidence, not a current on-view claim.',
170:'Damours Journal des Demoiselles 1982.152.20.83 is the 21.7×16.3 cm intaglio print of two dresses, including a wedding dress. The source circa-1840 date remains qualified; the title’s eighth annual series and twelfth issue do not create additional records.',
171:'Damours / Levert Journal des Demoiselles 1988.154.2 is the 23×15.3 cm color intaglio print with verso 1847 pencil notation and a dealer-lot stamp. Circa 1847 stays qualified, and the dollar amount/lot numbers are neither creation dates nor multiple artworks.',
172:'Damours Petit Courrier des Dames K3098 is the 23.4×15 cm color intaglio plate 1398, natively dated 15 November 1837. The caption names clothing retailers and makers; these remain description context rather than invented visual-artist authorities. It is one two-figure fashion print.',
173:'Jules David K1343 is the 28.3×23 cm watercolor/ink/graphite drawing for Moniteur de la Mode plate 340, physically signed Jules David and dated 4 August 1852. Its native object page explicitly identifies Jean-Baptiste David (1808–1892), resolving the national export’s incompatible 1848–1923 biography. Preserve both source labels and use a supported object-level maker derivation; no artist biography or authority record is edited.',
174:'Deferneville Journal des Demoiselles plate 5053, 1981.138.46.6, is one 32.7×24.8 cm color intaglio sheet natively dated 1 August 1895, with matching pencil year and Vallier gift inscription. Its different plate number, year and depicted dresses distinguish it from plate 5001 despite identical sheet size.'
})
