NOTES.update({
176:'Defraine / Duhamel Magasin des modes 2012.0.39 is one mounted color intaglio plate, 12×19.5 cm with separate 16.5×21 cm mount, plate 2 of the 22nd cahier, second year. Circa 1787 stays qualified. DOUBLE, Ville de Paris and Carnavalet stamps record object history; the native collection is Galliera and the 2015 exhibition is past display.',
177:'Delvaux / Lanté Costume Parisien K2333 is color intaglio plate 3089, 21.5×13.5 cm, natively dated 20 June 1833. The 1994 acquisition and verso pencil numeral remain separate from creation and object count.',
178:'Desrais Négligé uni K4249 is the 24.9×17.9 cm pen-and-wash drawing on laid paper glued to card with a separate old mount and cut caption. The description quotes a related engraved version credited to Dupin; that engraving is not the held drawing. Eighteenth-century dating remains broad.',
179:'Duhamel / François Louis Joseph Watteau de Lille 2025.21.32 is the 29×20.7 cm color intaglio print of a woman with parasol and two bonnets, circa 1775. It is not attributed to Antoine Watteau. Explicit June 1983 movement and March 2025 reassignment resolve historical Carnavalet stamps.',
180:'Dupin / Desrais Habit de Voltaire 2025.21.10 is plate 100, a 29×23.1 cm color intaglio print. The title includes 1778 while both exported and native dating say circa 1775; retain the discrepancy and full eighteenth-century eligibility bounds without inventing an exact year. G182 and old museum stamps are historical inventory context, with the 1983/2025 transfer explicitly documented.',
181:'Dupin / Le Clerc Chemise à la Reine 2025.21.86 is color intaglio plate 239, 41×26 cm, circa 1785. The back-view composition and larger sheet distinguish it from other dress plates. The native page explicitly documents the 1983 movement and 2025 reassignment from Carnavalet to Galliera.',
182:'Dupin / Watteau de Lille 2025.21.33 is the 29×20.5 cm circa-1775 print of a woman in a Pierrot caraco with additional bonnets and hats. It differs in composition from Duhamel’s parasol plate. Historical Carnavalet stamps are reconciled by the documented 1983/2025 transfer.',
183:'Esnault children’s fashion plate 4839, K4141, is the 28.5×19.8 cm color intaglio print with verso/recto dating evidence recorded natively as circa 1891. Several children share one sheet; no exact date or acquisition is invented.',
186:'Imprimerie Engelmann Conseiller des Dames 1952.5.134 is the 32.5×24.9 cm lithograph with gilding and black ink, dated 1890. The green fur-trimmed and black/mauve costumes share one print. Carnavalet stamps and Sailly’s 1952 gift remain provenance while the native page identifies Galliera as collection.',
187:'La Corbeille plate 883, 1982.152.20.20, is the 24.5×19.5 cm watercolor-enhanced intaglio print dated 1 June 1868. Héloïse Colin-Leloir is illustrator; the illegibly signed engraver remains unknown. Brayer’s 1982 donation is acquisition.',
188:'Gustave Janet Toilette de Bal plate 146, 1956.23.2.1, is a 35.3×27.2 cm lithograph, circa 1886, from a group of twenty Boué Sœurs fashion prints. The individually numbered sheet and native print description support one object; no bound-volume or twenty-object claim is inferred. Carnavalet and Boué Sœurs stamps and the 1956 donation remain evidence.',
189:'Jeannest-Gimel / Premet / Madame Grès 2013.39.10 is explicitly one card-stock sheet drawn and painted on the recto, 26×21 cm, dated by the Premet 12 January 1928 stamp. The logical ensemble comprises 91 drawings, not 91 counted works here. Draughtswoman, fashion-house and model-designer roles remain distinct; this gray/violet design differs from 2013.39.35.',
190:'Jeannest-Gimel / Premet / Madame Grès Laure 2013.39.35 is explicitly one 26×21 cm card-stock sheet, stamped Premet 12 July 1928. Its almond-green and white dress is a different design from 2013.39.10. The uncertain Jeannet? signature stays qualified, and the 91-drawing ensemble is context rather than a multiplied count.',
191:'Deux figures aux ombrelles 1987.131.43 is one trimmed 15.8×11 cm color intaglio print glued to a 19.5×14 cm laid-paper mount with a fisherman sketch and typescript on its reverse. These attached components describe one physical object, not separate artworks. Circa 1830 and publisher-only creator evidence remain.',
192:'Costume Parisien plate 73, 2025.21.132, is the anonymous 21×13.5 cm color intaglio print natively dated 28 October 1798. The Liesville/Carnavalet stamps agree with the documented 1881 gift, June 1983 movement and March 2025 reassignment. Historical custody is not a current-display claim.',
193:'B.C Journal des Demoiselles plate 5207, K3175, is the 32.4×25 cm color lithograph dated 15 October 1899, with Coursaget 2 pencil notation and 1996 gift. The assorted coiffures share one sheet; B.C remains unexpanded initials.',
194:'B.C / de Casimacker plate 4540, 1981.138.46.24, is the 26.5×19 cm color intaglio print dated 1 October 1885, with Don Vallier 1981 inscription. The individually inventoried headwear plate is distinct from plate 5207 by number, production, process and format.',
195:'B.C / Deferneville travestissement plate 4971bis, K3163, is the 32.7×24.8 cm color intaglio print dated 1 January 1894. The named Russian, Pierrette, Paquerette and Incroyable costumes are figures within one print, not four records.',
196:'B.C plate 5131, 1981.138.46.12, is the 32.5×25 cm color intaglio print dated 15 February 1897. Its turquoise/orange/pink figures, plate number and Vallier inventory distinguish it from plate 5127; the illegibly signed illustrator remains unknown.',
197:'B.C / Paul Lacourière plate 5127, 1981.138.46.14, is the 32.5×25 cm color intaglio print dated 1 January 1897. The distinct turquoise dotted, pink striped and green/white costumes distinguish its composition from plate 5131 despite shared format and donor.',
200:'Esnault / Deferneville plate 5071, 1981.138.46.4, is the 32.7×24.8 cm color intaglio print dated 1 December 1895, with pencil year and Don Vallier 1981 inscription. Native designer/engraver roles are retained; its mauve, blue and pink costume composition differs from the other Vallier sheets.'
})
