NOTES.update({
351:'Kerguenou Ernest Renan CSR S4 is the signed 52×49×39 cm patinated plaster portrait imitating terracotta, with the sitter’s eyes closed. Preserve actual plaster rather than changing medium to the imitated terracotta; Henry Scheffer’s portrait is a different maker/medium.',
352:'Elisa Kirchner La Bonne mère CSR AG70.2 is the signed 37.5×50.5 cm calligraphic drawing in pen, ink and watercolor on laid paper, dated 1 January 1818 and bearing a star watermark. French poem and English translation occupy one artwork. Gravelot’s much smaller 1765 ink drawing is a different maker and object.',
353:'La Tour Portrait du maréchal de Saxe D89.48 is the 65.5×54.2 cm pastel, circa 1748, with separate 81×70 cm frame and 1923 Lauth-Sand bequest. The sitter wears armor and the blue sash. Preserve qualified dating and compare Latour/La Tour and shortened Maurice de Saxe titles before final approval.',
355:'Lecouturier Augustin Thierry 2001.3 is the 27.4×19 cm paper engraving after Henry Scheffer, with sculp./pinx. and Delamain printing credits. Etex’s existing marble portrait 81.3.1 is a different medium and maker; the print’s production remains nineteenth century.',
356:'Lemoine Adolphe Guéroult 88.22.1 is the 31.7×23.2 cm lithograph after Pierson’s photograph, with separate image/frame sizes and Lemercier credit. The normalized accession collision 1882.2.1 belongs to an anonymous seventeenth-century oil-on-wood kitchen scene with different measurements; it is not the portrait print.',
358:'Mariani Conte di Trapani CSR AG68 is the 53×38 cm paper lithograph with G. Mariani dis, G. Riccio lit, Cirelli and Naples establishment dry-stamp credits. Include Riccio/Cirelli comparisons without inventing expanded identities. Related portraits of other Bourbon family members are distinct images, not this armored male sitter.',
359:'René Marjolin Etude d’enfant CSR AG240 is the 11.5×8.5 cm graphite drawing of a standing child in a shirt, annotated pompeia. Existing Thomas sanguine, Greuze canvas and Lobrichon wood paintings are different maker/media objects despite shared generic title.',
360:'Motte Mme Allan 2003.3.1 is the 27.5×21.6 cm lithograph of Marie Dorval as Thérèse, with C. Motte credit and Hte L stamp. It is distinct from the Noël/Kaeppelin seated portrait. The play’s 1822 premiere is contextual, not an exact production date imposed on the print.',
361:'Georgia S reproduction D89.45 is the 33.3×26 cm photomechanical print after Alfred de Musset’s portrait drawing. The native source explicitly dates the reproduction to the nineteenth century and distinguishes model authorship. Keep unknown reproduction maker and source period; do not misclassify it as Musset’s original drawing.',
362:'Mélingue Sainte Cécile 2022.4.1 is the signed 41×19×15 cm patinated bronze statuette playing a portable organ, circa 1842. It is distinct from Gechter’s smaller viol-playing bronze by maker, instrument and dimensions. Source physical-object dating remains qualified; no current-display claim follows the old exhibition field.',
363:'Célestin Nanteuil Théophile Gautier 88.19.1 is the 18.3×13.9 cm lithograph with Galerie de la Presse title, signature and Aubert printing/publishing credits. Aubert is the firm, not painter Augustin Aubert. The sitter’s bust with loose hair identifies one print, pending supplemental shortened-title comparison.',
364:'Léon Noël Mademoiselle Déjazet 2002.8 is the signed 28.5×20 cm lithograph for L’Artiste, explicitly lithographed from life. Its actress in straw hat and gigot sleeves differs from Marie Dorval; separate mount and 2002 acquisition remain evidence.',
365:'Léon Noël / Frey Alexandre Dumas 88.15.1 is the 25.5×18.5 cm lithograph for L’Artiste, with explicit from-life and printing inscriptions. Preserve the generic printed name and native Dumas fils indexing separately without resolving the sitter from assumption. This is distinct from Fuhr’s photograph-based Dumas portrait by maker and format.'
})
