NOTES.update({
366:'Princess d’Orléans Jetée de roses et de pensées 2013.11.2 is the watercolor/gouache on parchment signed and dated 27 February 1818. The 31×23 cm measurements describe the image and the 59.7×50.6 cm frame is separate. Flowers and butterfly form one still-life drawing; gallery labels remain provenance.',
367:'Perrier La Fuite en Egypte CSR AG277 is the 26.1×38.5 cm engraving on card-stock paper with separate mount, circa 1630. Repeated creator names represent engraver and model-author roles of the same supplied person. The embarking Holy Family composition remains distinct from other makers’ same-title works; English-title impressions require supplemental comparison before approval.',
368:'Ferdinand-Philippe 2013.11.3 is the 47.2×17.5×13.6 cm iron sculpture with silvering, gilding, patina and marble. The native page attributes only the model to James Pradier; preserve both attribution and model role, leaving execution unknown. Nineteenth-century physical-object production is separate from the sitter’s life dates and 2013 purchase.',
369:'Pradier Femme lavant ses longs cheveux 2002.17 is the 29.3×15×11 cm patinated bronze with Pradier and Susse Frères signatures. Susse is the founder; the obsolete Femme tressant title remains an alternative, not another work. Retain source nineteenth-century production without inventing a casting year.',
370:'Puvis de Chavannes Tête de Sainte Geneviève CSR AG27 is the signed 31×23.5 cm charcoal/white-chalk drawing on laid paper. Existing Puvis gouache GL4464 is much smaller and on another medium, his oil study is a different composition, and Hébert’s exact-title lead has another maker. The separate mount is not part of work dimensions.',
371:'Ary Renan Etude de trois figures 93.1.25 is the 28×17 cm sanguine drawing on tracing paper, monogrammed AR. One woman and two men share one sheet; antique dress is subject matter, not creation chronology. The 1993 acquisition is separate.',
372:'Emile Rouargue after Adolphe Rouargue Mr Arnal 2003.3.11 is Galerie Théâtrale plate 35, 28.3×21.6 cm, with explicit Dessiné/Gravé credits. Preserve the Anténor/Antenov source-title variants and broad nineteenth-century date; the 1833 theatrical premiere is context.',
373:'George Sand Portrait de Sophie Delaborde D89.71 is the 18×16 cm graphite/charcoal drawing on wove paper, with old inventory D8345 and separate mount. The sitter is the artist’s mother; the 1923 bequest and historical accession remain evidence, not creation.',
374:'Maurice Sand La Foire Saint-Laurent D89.16 is the graphite/wash/gouache paper drawing of an animated fair, with a Liesville stamp and 1953 bequest. Native work dimensions are 26.7×42.4 cm while the national export additionally gives 24.3×40; retain both measurements and their source context without inventing a correction. Maurice remains distinct from George Sand.',
375:'George Sand enfant D89.96 is the 20×15 cm pastel attributed to Aurore de Saxe, circa 1810. Native Attribué à and the old Carnavalet label must remain. The native narrative’s family relationship is not imported as biography; the child in white with blue ribbon differs from adult George Sand portraits.',
376:'Ary Scheffer Les Saintes Femmes 90.1.1 is the signed, squared 26.5×21 cm graphite study on tracing paper, circa 1846. Manchester’s 1845 painting, Cornelia’s lava tomb painting and Oscar Gué’s large oil canvas are distinct physical works; the preparatory drawing keeps its own date qualification.',
377:'Gihaut after Ary Scheffer Souvenirs d’un soldat CSR AG17 is the 24×28.1 cm paper lithograph, numbered 8 in the margin and bearing the plural printed title. Native model/lithographer roles are preserved; the series number counts no additional objects.',
378:'Henry Scheffer La famille royale prisonnière CSR AG20 is the 7.1×13 cm graphite/gouache/ink drawing on card-stock paper. It is an original design related to Thiers’s history, physically distinct from Goulu’s larger Tuileries engraving; the book’s first publication span is contextual.',
380:'Sixdeniers after Charpentier Rachel JC95.42 is the 56×42.4 cm paper mezzotint with Chardon/Giraudet printing credit and Rachel’s handwritten dedication dated 10 March 1842. Model painter, engraver, printer and sitter stay distinct. The separate mount and broad exported nineteenth-century date remain.'
})
