NOTES.update({
381:'Julien Tinayre Portrait présumé de George Sand 2003.3.9 is the 52.5×39.5 cm chine/card-stock lithograph, signed Julien Tinayre and numbered 40/6. Preserve the presumed sitter in the title; the fraction is not a held-edition count. Different-maker Charpentier portrait does not identify this print.',
382:'Anaïs Toudouze / Sarazin CSR AG59 is the 25×16 cm engraving for Magasin des Demoiselles, signed and dated 25 July 1866, showing two women and a girl in a garden. Sarazin is the printer. The subscription price and related-plate link are contextual, not extra works or creation dates.',
384:'Vincenzo Vela Main de femme 2002.7 is the signed 13.5×26×34 cm marble of a ringed left hand on an embroidered cushion, circa 1860. The sculpted cushion is integrated into one work; it differs materially and compositionally from the plaster hand casts and Scheffer’s marble hand on a simple base.',
385:'Deux femmes auprès d’un perroquet CSR AG41 is the 26.3×20.5 cm watercolor/gouache/graphite drawing attributed to Princess Sophia Zamoyska. Preserve explicit native attribution and the proposed Vert-vert literary reference as uncertain; the poem/opera/related painting dates do not replace source nineteenth-century production.',
386:'Anonymous Gustave Flaubert 88.10.1 is the 11.7×7 cm paper drypoint, monogrammed LL and titled G. FLAUBERT. The initials are not expanded to an invented maker. Separate frame measurements and the 1988 acquisition remain distinct from creation.',
387:'Projet de tombeau pour Marie d’Orléans 88.33.1 is the 32.6×46 cm paper lithograph after Achille Devéria, inscribed A.DEVERIA.1839 with Lemercier-Bernard credit. It is the printed project, not the represented tomb or sculptures; retain broad exported dating and literal frame dimensions even though width/orientation differs.',
388:'Marthe et Marguerite CSR AG34 is the 19×23.2 cm graphite/gouache drawing on wove paper by an anonymous hand after Scheffer. The reverse asks Copie par un élève ?; preserve the copy/model relationship and uncertain pupil status. The future exhibition listing is not current display.',
389:'Joséphine Calamatta Portrait de Lina D89.104 is the round 20 cm pencil/wash drawing with old D8349 and Lauth-Sand labels, in a separate circular frame. Luigi Calamatta’s 48.5×33 cm charcoal portrait of Lina is a different maker and format; Joséphine’s Luigi portrait depicts another sitter.',
390:'Clésinger D89.178 is the original 5×13.5×24 cm plaster cast of Chopin’s left hand with old inventory S3332. Preserve native 1847–1849 alongside exported circa 1849, without silent rewrite. The linked cancelled/reassigned Carnavalet notice represents a transfer and requires supplemental old-inventory/native-URL comparison before adding.',
391:'David d’Angers Franz Liszt D89.22 is the 18.6 cm diameter, 1.5 cm thick plaster medallion inscribed F Liszt. Retain nineteenth-century object production and the separate 1923 bequest; the sitter’s life dates do not date the cast. Compare shortened Liszt titles before final approval.',
392:'Gihaut after Ary Scheffer Le vieux pâtre CSRAG19 is the 31.3×23.5 cm lithograph numbered 3 in the series, with Scheffer/Gihaut inscriptions. Native CSR AG 19 is the spaced form of the same accession. One numbered sheet is counted, not the whole series.',
393:'Mariani Conte di Aquila CSR AG69 is the 52.5×38 cm paper lithograph with Riccio/Cirelli credits and Naples dry stamp. The named sitter Luigi differs from the Count of Trapani, despite matching armored-portrait format and publishing house; related family portraits stay separate.',
394:'René Marjolin Femme accoudée à sa porte CSR AG243 is the 24.3×16.3 cm graphite drawing on laid paper with La Stella watermark. The Italian mother watching her drinking child is one travel-study sheet, distinct from the much smaller isolated child study CSR AG240.',
395:'Puvis de Chavannes Tête d’homme CSR AG028 is the large 59×49.5 cm charcoal drawing on tracing paper of a bearded man in a headscarf. Existing inventory 158 is a 16×12.5 cm white-paper head, and 155/138/167 have other subjects, supports or format. Native inventory omits the leading zero as CSR AG28; preserve both literal forms with a specific comparison exception.',
396:'Ary Renan Etude pour Armide 93.1.28 is the 29×23 cm graphite drawing of a crowned woman near a garden fountain, marked AR and Armide. The supplied passe-partout Profondeur 40 cm is a source anomaly retained without changing it to width. Production remains nineteenth century.',
397:'George Sand Portrait de Maurice en officier D89.72 is the 12.2×9 cm graphite/watercolor drawing with George and Solange inscriptions, republican caption and old D8342/E19501 labels. Preserve the second signature as evidence without inventing joint authorship; source assignment remains George, pending supplemental inscription-name comparison.',
398:'Maurice Sand Château neuf et château vieux D89.108 is the 15.4×17.6 cm graphite/chalk/ink drawing with gilding, linked to George Sand’s novel. Old D8353 and the identifying handwritten caption belong to one work. Preserve the erroneous-looking mount width 30 m literally; do not silently turn it into centimeters.',
399:'Ary Scheffer Main de Cornélia CSR S6 is the 24.8×14.8×8 cm marble hand on a simple quadrangular base. Henry Scheffer’s portrait of Cornélia is another maker and object form, and Vela’s ringed hand on a cushion is another sculptor/composition.',
400:'Anonymous Théophile Gautier 88.11.1 is the aquatint with 15.8×10 cm plate mark, Bayard inscription and a caricatural full-length rear-three-quarter view. Include Bayard in comparison without expanding the signature to a guessed authority. It differs from Nanteuil’s bust lithograph by process, composition and format.'
})
