NOTES.update({
401:'David d’Angers George Sand 2000.LUB.2235 is the 16 cm diameter, 1.2 cm thick bronze medallion with the sitter’s vertical inscription. Same-sitter Vieusseux oil painting and Julien/Couture prints are different forms and makers. Preserve nineteenth-century source cast dating and separate 2000 donation.',
402:'Marjolin Femme des environs de Rome CSR AG244 is the 26.4×18.9 cm graphite drawing on watermarked laid paper, showing a seated woman from behind with braided hair. Retain the uncertain Terracino/Terracina inscription literally; the different pose and sheet distinguish it from other travel studies.',
403:'Ary Renan Etude pour Pandora 93.1.30 is the 31×21 cm graphite drawing with AR monogram and blue-pencil notes specifying Pandora, a dove and Epimetheus with flute. The notes describe a planned painting, not a second held artwork; preserve the separate mount and nineteenth-century period.',
405:'Maurice Sand Gargilesse D89.111 is the 31.2×46 cm graphite drawing on wove paper physically dated 14 October 1844, with old D8356 and the novel-related title inscription. The panorama and separate frame establish one original sheet; no novel publication date is substituted.',
406:'Effie et Jeanie CSR AG6 is the 33.8×27.7 cm watercolor/iron-gall-ink/gouache drawing attributed to Ary Scheffer. Preserve explicit attribution and reverse alternative La jeune captive as comparison evidence. The Walter Scott narrative identifies the prison scene but does not supply creation year.',
407:'Anonymous Louis Viardot 88.26.1 is the lithograph for Galerie de la Presse, with 23.2×17.4 cm plate mark and Aubert printing/publishing inscriptions. Louis remains distinct from Pauline and Louis Ratisbonne; unknown designer stays unknown despite printer credits.',
408:'Marjolin Femme tenant son enfant CSR AG248 is the 19×13.7 cm graphite sheet on laid paper with La Stella watermark. The seated mother holding a sleeping child differs from the doorway scene and isolated child, with its own described physical sheet.',
409:'Ary Renan Etude d’un couple 93.1.34 is the signed 21×11.3 cm pencil drawing, squared for transfer, of two people holding hands. It remains one original study with broad source nineteenth-century date; no finished painting is inferred.',
410:'George Sand Pauline Viardot JC95.21 is the small 9.5×10 cm graphite drawing on wove paper signed G. Sand, with separate mount and frame. Existing Ary Scheffer and Maurice Sand portraits have different named makers; the signature supports retaining George’s object-level identity rather than merging on sitter.',
411:'Maurice Sand L’Etna 2000.6 is the 17.5×23.5 cm watercolor landscape made with dendritic impressions. Preserve the uncertain identification of the snowy summit as Etna. Narrative dates for George Sand’s practice are not imposed on Maurice’s drawing, and mount/frame sizes remain separate.',
412:'Deux femmes dans un paysage CSR AG8 is the 39.4×26 cm watercolor attributed to Ary Scheffer. Preserve both native Attribué à and reverse Marguerite et la Vierge ? uncertainty. Its kneeling woman and visiting saint differ from the two sisters in the prison scene; future exhibition is not current display.',
413:'Anonymous Paysage de Juan-les-Pins D89.42 is the 15×25 cm graphite drawing of people under coastal pines. Preserve the incomplete 18[?] and unknown-author inscription with source nineteenth-century dating. Picasso’s larger 1920 oil canvas is a different medium/date object.',
414:'Marjolin Coiffure de femme de Frosinone CSR AG251 is the 8.7×6.5 cm graphite hair study, inscribed frosinone. Its focus on multiple braids and small sheet distinguish it from the larger seated Roman woman drawing.',
415:'Ary Renan 93.1.5 is explicitly one double-sided 17.2×24 cm pen/ink/graphite sheet: draped women, silhouette and hand studies on the recto, landscape on the verso. The five figures and reverse study do not become separate records.',
416:'George Sand Paysage imaginaire 2002.6 is the 15×23.5 cm dendritic watercolor landscape with two figures and a dog, whose identification as grandchildren/Fadet remains tentative. Existing Lallemand’s larger oil canvas is another maker/medium; Maurice’s Etna watercolor is a different assigned maker and scene.',
417:'Scène à l’Antique CSR AG12 is the 21.1×15.8 cm watercolor of two men attributed to Ary Scheffer, with separate 41×31 cm mount. Preserve the native attribution qualifier and unknown precise subject/date; antique dress is not ancient production.',
418:'Anonymous Aurore de Saxe et son fils D89.52 is the 48×58.7 cm pastel, circa 1785, with old D8333/E19481 and Carnavalet label. Two sitters share one landscape-format portrait; uncertain Tivoli architecture is not asserted as a verified location.',
419:'Marjolin Femme de Sora CSR AG252 is the 28.7×21.4 cm graphite drawing with written color instructions. Those words do not make the monochrome drawing a colored work; the standing back-view costume study differs from the Roman seated figure.',
420:'Ary Renan Etude de femme assise 93.1.6 is the 21.8×16 cm graphite drawing, monogrammed AR, showing the front-facing seated woman draped around head and shoulders. Its one figure and portrait format differ from the multi-figure double-sided study; broad nineteenth-century date stays.'
})
