NOTES.update({
456:'Vianden à travers une toile d’araignée inventory 83 is explicitly Victor Hugo’s detached sheet from travel album BnF NAF13349, 25.5×30.3 cm, natively dated 13 August 1871. Native creator attribution fills the national export’s missing maker at object level, preserving both sources. The remaining BnF album is not another holding of this detached sheet; joint Maison de Victor Hugo–Hauteville House collection does not establish physical display site.',
457:'Jules Adeline Un coin du cimetière de Villequier 603 is the signed 31.5×23 cm pen/ink/wash drawing marked Villequier September 1891. Dates on the depicted tombstones and the 1903 gift are not its creation; Méaulle’s related prints are different reproductions.',
459:'Auguste Belin Triboulet 303 is the signed oval 18×12.8 cm gouache/graphite drawing with separate museum mount and 1932 gift. Roybet’s existing 64×52 cm oil canvas has another maker and medium. The source expressly leaves this drawing’s historical context undocumented.',
460:'Les romantiques chassés du temple 2292.2 is one mounted 25.2×32.6 cm black-and-white lithograph, a separate Album théâtral edition of the December 1838 caricature. Preserve Benjamin Roubaud as current maker and de Barray as the former/pseudonymous credit, with Aubert printer and Bauger publisher. The title Album does not make this a bound volume; native text expressly identifies the separate edition.',
461:'Bichebois La Roche Guyon 2017.0.3720 is the 19.8×26.8 cm lithograph fixed to a mount, with separate 12.6×17 cm image and printed address. Repeated Bichebois entries are lithographer and printer roles. Pissarro’s portrait-format etching and Braque’s oil paintings are different maker/media objects.',
462:'Ernest-Philippe Boetzel Victor Hugo 3489 is the large 130×95 cm charcoal drawing on paper/canvas, natively dated 14 March 1885, with separate frame. Existing etchings, Rodin plaster and other makers’ portraits are not this monumental drawing. The 1927 donation is acquisition.',
463:'Julien Léopold Boilly Marie Nodier-Mennessier 2014.0.25 is the signed 27.8×21.5 cm lithograph printed by Thierry Frères, glued to a support in a separate museum mount. The support and mount measurements are not additional works; supplied maker identity remains distinct from other Boilly family members.',
464:'Boulanger Don Sallustre 294u is the 29.5×21 cm watercolor costume design for the 1838 Ruy Blas staging, with separate mount, color/material notes and verso 14. Its membership in the costume-design ensemble is logical; the independently mounted sheet counts once. It differs from Lucien Mélingue’s later Don César illustration by role, maker and date.',
465:'Boulanger / Motte Sara la baigneuse 405 is explicitly a proof before letters of the 1830 lithograph, with separate image/work/sheet/mount sizes. The native source distinguishes two other lettered impressions, providing print-state evidence rather than accession-only separation. The 1886 reuse is not the production date of this mounted proof.',
466:'Bouvenne Rocher des Proscrits 610 is the 43.4×29.8 cm etching after Charles Hugo’s photograph, dedicated to Madame Drouet and printed by Lemercier. Preserve before-1883 production with unknown lower bound; title 1852–1855 describes the photographic subject period, not an exact print date.',
467:'Bouvenne Frauenfeld 2017.0.726 is the 42.8×31.3 cm etching after Victor Hugo’s separately held drawing inventory 2. Native before-1900 production remains exclusive; February 1900 dedication to Paul Meurice is separate evidence. The print and its original model are not interchangeable.',
468:'Jean Valjean et Cosette 2337 is the 20.4×12.8 cm etching after Gustave Brion, natively dated 1862 and fixed in a passe-partout. The engraver remains anonymous. Jean Geoffroy’s later illustration has another supplied maker/date; the literary characters do not identify the same physical work.',
469:'Jean Bulio Victor Hugo 1468 is the signed 16×10.4×6.9 cm bronze bust on a pedestal. The unbearded sitter’s presumed pre-exile appearance does not replace the source nineteenth-century production date. Rodin’s much larger plaster and various portrait prints are different works.',
470:'Léon Cuisinier Légende des siècles 2017.0.3111.1 is the signed 22.3×13.7 cm etching mounted under a passe-partout, with separate image/plate measurements. Preserve possible frontispiece status and unknown publisher. It is the described mounted print, not the book or an asserted entire edition.'
})
