NOTES.update({
471:'Vincenzo D’Amore Victor Hugo 1715 is the ochre-patinated plaster bust, 30×24.5×18.5 cm with separate 8 cm pedestal, signed at the left shoulder cut. Retain nineteenth-century object dating and the circa-1870 represented appearance separately. The native ambiguous avant 1855 maker date must not become an asserted birth year; Rodin’s 57 cm plaster and Bulio’s small bronze are physically different works.',
473:'Gustave Deloye Victor Hugo 1483 is the signed 66×46×27 cm plaster bust explicitly documented as the Salon 1881 no.3800 plaster. Source production 1881 is distinct from the circa-1903 Beuve gift. Maker, monumental size and exhibition identity distinguish other portrait busts.',
474:'Achille Devéria Madame Hugo 647 is the signed graphite portrait, circa 1823, donated by Jacques Devéria in 1927. Preserve the national 27.1×208 cm and 52.8×37.8 cm measurements and the native 52.8×37.8 cm work measurement as conflicting evidence; do not silently correct 208 or reassign measurement roles. The exact inventory, title, signature and source link identify the sheet despite that dimension anomaly.',
476:'Personnages des romans 2017.0.2645 is one unmounted 24.8×33.3 cm wood engraving signed H. Vogel / E. Dété. Its twelve characters from five novels share one sheet. Preserve Vogel’s designer/model role and Dété’s engraving role, with broad nineteenth-century production.',
477:'Menu du Banquet Grisel 2017.0.4084 is one unmounted illustrated 24×15.8 cm photomechanical print dated 10 May 1882, with E. Dietrich publishing address. Count the described graphic sheet once. Dietrich is publisher only; keep the conflicting Marianne versus Frédérick Lemaître iconographic descriptions as source uncertainty without assigning either as a verified depicted person or maker.',
478:'Claude Escholier La Rose de l’Infante 758 is the signed 73.5×54.2 cm oil canvas illustrating La Légende des siècles, with separate frame. Native before-1936 production is distinct from acquisition in 1936 and leaves the lower bound unknown. Preserve the native after-1979 maker-life qualifier without changing an artist authority.',
479:'Eugène Sue 2011.0.37 is a 22×16.2 cm etching cut from La France Maritime and glued to a support in a separate mount. Printed Ferdinand sc. and Couxeley del. remain literal role evidence; do not expand the incomplete names. The Balzac lithograph of Sue differs in printing process and size, while the former book is not counted as another artwork.',
480:'Gagne Catafalque de Victor Hugo sous l’Arc de Triomphe 314 is the signed 46.2×31.4 cm watercolor physically dated 31 May 1885, agreeing with native exact production. Preserve the incompatible native third-quarter-century category as an anomaly, without overriding the explicit date. Falguière’s depicted monument is not the maker of this drawing.',
481:'Auguste Garnier / Aimé de Lemud Notre-Dame frontispiece 2705 is a China-paper steel-engraving proof glued to a 28.8×19.7 cm paper support and fixed in a mount, dated 1844. Distinguish Garnier engraver, Lemud model designer, Dien printer and Perrotin publisher. Its separately described mounted proof differs from the 1836 Johannot and 1893 Benjamin-Constant illustrations; no whole book is inferred.',
482:'Hippolyte-Louis Garnier Leicester et Amy Robsart 2017.0.3844 is the mounted color lithograph, work 58.8×44.8 cm and image 44.5×36.8 cm. Printed Oct. Tassaert invt., Garnier lith. and Villain printer identify separate creative and printing roles. The broad nineteenth-century date stays; an unknown publisher is not invented.',
483:'Jules-Arsène Garnier Voyage en Espagne 704 is the 20×16.5 cm pen/black-ink drawing with separate old mount and frame. The depicted journey in Hugo’s youth is subject matter, not execution dating. Preserve broad nineteenth-century production and the untranscribed signature without supplying a precise year.',
484:'Gherardi Victor E. Hugo 1746 is the 34×19.5×14.5 cm plaster bust natively dated 1882. Preserve the incomplete physical signature Gherardi 188. literally, distinct from the catalogue’s date, and the circa-1903 gift. Other plaster busts differ in maker and dimensions.',
485:'Adèle Hugo Léopoldine 512 is the 24×20 cm graphite drawing signed and dated 4 January 1843, with separate mount and frame. The wedding context does not replace the physical date. Duvidal’s portrait of the sitter as an infant has another maker and depicted age.'
})
