NOTES.update({
486:'François-Victor Hugo La Mort de Messaline de Louis Boulanger 4372 is the 19.8×31.6 cm pen/ink drawing dated 1843. Preserve the satirical Victor Hugo en la personne de son fils wording as an inscription, not a reassignment from the explicitly named son to his father or to the mocked painter Boulanger. One mounted sheet counts once.',
487:'Léopold Armand Hugo Le Général Hugo 591.1 is a mounted 44×35.5 cm engraving with a 40×31.5 cm plate mark. Duplicate author entries represent draughtsman and engraver roles. Julie DUVIDAL P.1826 is a printed model credit, not an exact production date for the later engraving; retain nineteenth-century object dating and compare the model-maker spelling.',
488:'Victor Hugo Rencontre en mer 140 is the 25.4×19.3 cm wash drawing on wove paper, physically inscribed 26 October 1864 and linked to the witnessed overturned ship. Preserve the obsolete La Tempête title for comparison. Same-title works by Vernet, Ziem, Ruisdael, Strub, Scheffer and Rivière have different named makers, production periods or media; related Méaulle reproductions remain separate from the signed original.',
489:'Le Chat et le village de Saint-Goarshausen / Le Reichenberg 2017.0.753.2 is one 24.9×31.5 cm photomechanical facsimile sheet fixed in an old mount. Hugo is model author, Grégoire et Deneux printer and Challamel publisher. Its two reproduced BnF manuscript drawings do not become two held originals; preserve the source impossible 31 September model date as evidence without importing it as print production.',
490:'Marie-Edmond Höner Victor Hugo 638 is the 20 cm diameter ink/gouache tondo signed E.Höner 1897, showing the face in a starry sky above Notre-Dame. The two separate mounts do not enlarge the artwork count. Its format, composition and maker distinguish generic Hugo portrait prints and busts.',
491:'Unknown maker Renée-Louise Trébuchet 216 is the 81.3×65 cm eighteenth-century oil canvas with separate gilt frame. The museum expressly pairs it with a different portrait of her husband inventory 215. Preserve unreadable-signature attribution; sitter life dates and 1903 gift are not substituted for creation.',
492:'Poème d’Etienne Carjat 2017.0.3115 is one 25×32.2 cm mounted photomechanical graphic sheet with portrait/text and the inscription Etienne mal croqué par Emile. Carjat is the text author; the visual creator remains unknown and Emile is not expanded to an authority. Preserve nineteenth-century object date and unknown printer/publisher.',
493:'Maison rue Notre-Dame-des-Champs 2011.0.35 is the 28.2×21.9 cm mounted photomechanical reproduction, with Just-Simon signature and original-model relation to Régnier/Champin. Preserve the conflicting 1822 caption versus 1827–1830 subject index as contextual evidence, not production dating. Native maker remains unknown pending signature reconciliation.',
494:'Aux morts de Juillet 2017.0.3078 is the 29×20.3 cm wood engraving fixed in a separate mount, signed ELWAL. Preserve the literal signature and unknown maker rather than invent a full identity. The poem identification is literary subject, not a precise print date; broad nineteenth-century production remains.',
495:'Tony Johannot Juliette Drouet as Marie in Périnet Leclerc 305 is the 21.5×15.5 cm watercolor dated 1832, with separate mount and uncertain Philippe Burty collector mark. The related Darondeau Charivari print is another maker/medium; do not treat the 1481 dramatic setting or 1933 acquisition as production.',
496:'Joliet / Chifflart Anniversaire de la mort de Victor Hugo 2016.0.75.1 is the 58.1×41.1 cm wood-engraved double page fixed in a museum mount. Its explicit 1886 publication and before-the-Panthéon allegorical composition distinguish the 1868 portrait and 1885 death event. Count one physical double sheet, preserving designer, engraver and publisher roles.',
497:'Kreutzberger Eternelle lumière de la pensée 2017.0.3937 is one unmounted 25×27.2 cm lithograph bearing the designer’s signature and E3372 inscription. Its 29 writer portraits are one composition, not 29 artworks. Preserve Thiébault’s photographer role and Girard/Boitte publishing role separately from lithographer.',
498:'Désiré-François Laugée Victor Hugo sur son lit de mort 615 is the 37×61 cm pencil drawing signed D.L. and dated 24 May 1885, with three manuscripts and a rosebud attached to its mount. The native page separately relates Glaize’s work; Laugée’s signed drawing is not merged with Glaize or Bonnat solely on sitter/date. Count the drawing and associated mount as one catalogue object.',
500:'Lepeudry Victor Hugo et Alexandre Dumas 441.1 is the complete 26.4×35.7 cm lithographed double plate, numbers 9 and 10, with two caricature busts and shared credits, dated 1833. Count the complete sheet once. The additional cut-Hugo record 3146 bears the same old 441.1 and remains held until physical object history resolves whether it is a separate impression.'
})
