NOTES.update({
501:'Auguste Lepère Fête du centenaire 312 is the 40×57 cm watercolor/gouache/pastel/ink drawing dated March 1902, in a separate frame. The night celebration on the Place des Vosges is one composition; Hugo’s residence dates and the October 1935 acquisition are not creation dates.',
502:'Georges Loukomski Maison 30 rue du Dragon 301 is the 30×22 cm pencil drawing, explicitly before 1929, donated that year. Preserve the unknown lower creation bound and Russian maker’s supplied spelling; Hugo’s 1821–1822 residence dates are subject history, not drawing production.',
503:'Georges-Henri Manesse Rue du Cherche Midi 37 2017.0.3673 is the mounted etching with M signature and old MVHPEREC1154 inscription. Preserve literal 23.2×26.5 cm work and 22×26.8 cm plate measurements despite the width anomaly. The titled building and separately labeled mount identify one print; broad nineteenth-century production stays.',
504:'Marietti L’Homme qui pense 1520 is the signed 23×12.8×9 cm patinated plaster bust, dated 1878. The native page cites the sculptor’s letter of 26 September 1878 attesting this bust’s gift, supporting the exact date despite an inconsistent third-quarter-century category. Preserve title, object history and later acquisition separately.',
506:'Maurin Victor Hugo 2017.0.439 is the mounted 36.4×28.2 cm lithograph with printed Chaillou and Lemercier credits and PC dry stamp. Native before-1837 production is consistent with the described early edition; its printed credits distinguish the later Rosselin/Formentin state, whose conflicting production date is held separately.',
507:'Méaulle Les tombes de la famille Hugo 2017.0.3726.1 is the 31.3×21 cm wood engraving after Adeline, circa 1891, with F.Méaulle sc. / Adeline Villequier sept91 signatures and old MVHPEREC1216. Its printing process distinguishes Adeline’s original pen/wash drawing candidate457; mount and image are components of one print.',
509:'Méaulle / Lançon Petit Paul 2017.0.3114 is the mounted 25×17.5 cm wood engraving, with separately measured image and both printed signatures. Retain designer and engraver roles and unknown printer/publisher. The poem number does not imply multiple physical works or an exact production year.',
511:'Nanteuil Mme Dorval as Catarina Bragadini 429.1 is the 25.5×18.4 cm lithograph signed and dated 1835, with separate museum mount and old INV423/Cat652. Native publication history explains use as an inserted plate; the held print is individually described, not a whole journal or play volume.',
512:'Nargeot / Gerlier Les compères du Roy 2017.0.2671 is the 25.2×15.9 cm mounted wood engraving marked T46, No4, Gerlier del. and Nargeot sc. One literary scene occupies the sheet. Preserve native Nargeot after-1865 biography qualification at object-label level and retain broad nineteenth-century print production.',
514:'Pellerin Silhouettes Général Hugo et Victor Hugo 2011.0.18.1 is one 42.4×31.8 cm printed image fixed to a larger sheet and mount. The cutout instructions and finished silhouette examples share one intact graphic sheet, not several objects. Preserve printed series1383 versus commentary1883 discrepancy; repeated Pellerin entries are printer and publisher roles.',
515:'Pilotell Vision du poète 2041 is the large 132×99.5 cm charcoal drawing on paper, before 1883, in a separate frame. The museum distinguishes this original from Goupil reproductions in the Livre d’or; other illustrators’ contributions to that book are different compositions and makers.'
})
