NOTES.update({
516:'Pilotell L’Enfer 499 is the large 55.4×83 cm lithograph signed 2 December 1868 and personally dedicated to Hugo, with Hugo at the left. The source distinguishes the right-side version inventory497. Preserve the uncertain relation to an April1868 diary entry, the mount-width anomaly and possible provenance as uncertainty; do not replace the physical signature date with that narrative.',
517:'Hippolyte Prudhomme Le Sylphe 2017.0.3092 is the mounted 25×30.2 cm etching with h.Prudhomme sc. and separate plate/image dimensions. Preserve the native draughtsman-lithographer role versus etching medium rather than invent another maker. The connection to Hugo’s poem is expressly tentative, while the museum holding and nineteenth-century production are explicit.',
518:'Armand Queyroy Maison du général Hugo à Blois 2015.0.28 is the 26.6×21.6 cm pencil drawing on brown paper, physically dated14May1864 and dedicated to Hugo. Correspondence identifies this original as a separate thank-you drawing from the Blois etching album. Preserve Quéroy as a comparison spelling, not another person.',
519:'Les Placards populaires 2017.0.3926.1 is one 54.7×35.7 cm wood-engraved broadside fixed in a mount, with Charles, Victor and François-Victor portraits. Preserve the separate engraver/designer/model/printer roles of its eight supplied credits. Three portraits on one sheet count once; Charaire and the publisher are not inferred painters.',
520:'Rochegrosse La cloche 453 is the signed 44.6×29.4 cm graphite/charcoal/gouache drawing on blue-gray paper, mounted on a hinge with old reverse repairs. Preserve native circa1885–1886 alongside export circa1886 as date evidence. It is the original model for Méaulle’s printed plate, distinct from Rochegrosse’s other literary subjects and the companion Canaris drawing.',
521:'Régereau La Chasse du burgave 2014.0.59.37 is explicitly an 18.6×24.8 cm trial print glued to card, stored in a portfolio of43 studies, circa1885. The separately described card-mounted proof is one physical unit; neither the portfolio count nor the depicted vignette number multiplies it. Retain the title spelling and unknown printing process.',
522:'Meyer / Smeeton and Tilly Les sénateurs de Paris 2089.6 is an unmounted 52×34 cm wood-engraved illustration explicitly extracted from the13February1876 Journal illustré. Five sitters occupy one graphic sheet. Preserve the named photographic models in the printed letter; the journal issue is not imported as a second whole object.',
523:'SGAP Le pilori 2017.0.2672 is the mounted 43.5×33.9 cm photomechanical print of Quasimodo and Esmeralda. The Société générale d’applications photographiques is photograver; the model artist remains unknown despite the unreadable-signature field. The literary chapter identifies the scene, not its physical production date.',
524:'Soupey / Daniel Vierge Le Sultan Mourad 2017.0.3118 is the 22.9×21.3 cm mounted wood engraving with 7.3 cm circular image and both signatures. Keep engraver, designer, Martinet printer and Hébert/Houssiaux publishers distinct. The1875 edition is evidence, while the supplied broad nineteenth-century date is retained.',
526:'Van Guell Maison de la corporation des peintres à Bruxelles 1351 is the signed 33.8×19.2 cm pen drawing on cream paper, hinged in a separate mount and bearing old E1028/I1351. Explicit before1950 production is consistent with the25January1950 gift; do not expand the maker’s incomplete name or replace it with Hugo’s residence date.',
527:'Anonymous Buste monté sur colonne 4312 is one double-sided 15.6×9.7 cm black-ink drawing on cream paper, with a sovereign skeleton on the reverse. VH collector/museum stamps are not an author signature. Preserve anonymity and count both sides as one nineteenth-century drawing.',
528:'Sara la baigneuse 2017.0.3071.2 is the 28.2×22.6 cm mounted photomechanical facsimile explicitly after Hanriot’s engraving of a Gautier drawing. Both are model authors; the photograver is anonymous. It differs from Boulanger/Motte’s1830 lithographic proof by source composition lineage and printing process.',
529:'Paysage à la barque 2017.0.720 is a 26×33.2 cm mounted wood engraving after Hugo, with VH within its6×8 cm image and old MVHPEREC1318. The native source locates the original drawing in Chenay’s bound exemplar inventory1287, distinguishing this independent print from that album component. The same-title Dupré lead has another supplied model maker; do not turn the anonymous print into an original Hugo drawing.',
530:'Anonymous Eugène Hugo 605.1 is the mounted17.6×14 cm lithograph printed by Lemercier/Bénard in1839, independently supported by the cited November1839 publication notice. It depicts a sculptural bust of Victor’s brother, not Victor himself or a held sculpture. Unknown designer remains unknown.'
})
